    questionRef: '2024 OL · Section 1 Q6(ii) · Marketing (Product Life Cycle)',
    stem: 'Using the product life cycle diagram above, identify a stage where the sales curve is decreasing.',
    commandWord: 'identify',
    demand: 'Trace the curve, then name the stage that sits over the falling part of it. On this diagram the line rises through Launch, Growth and the blank \'Stage 3\' box, reaches its peak around Saturation, then turns downward — so the decreasing stage to name is Decline (the scheme also accepts Saturation as the turning region). \'Identify\' wants the stage name only; no description of the stage is required or rewarded.',
    trap: 'Naming the wrong end of the curve. The peak — where sales are highest — sits around Saturation, so candidates who answer with the highest-sales stage have named where sales top out, not where they fall. The decreasing section is to the RIGHT of the peak, in Decline. Beware too of answering \'Stage 3\' (the blank highlighted box, which is part (i)\'s Maturity answer): Stage 3 is still on the rising part of the curve, so it is not a decreasing stage. The scheme accepts only Saturation / Decline; each part of Q6 is worth 5 of the 10 marks, so naming a rising stage loses the whole 5.',
    source: 'SEC LC Business 2024 OL marking scheme, Section 1 Q6: (i) stage 3 = Maturity 5m, (ii) stage where sales decrease = \'Saturation / Decline\' 5m',
