    commandWord: 'Define',
    demand: '\'Define\' wants a tight, self-contained statement of meaning - not an example, cause or consequence. The scheme rewards the two technical components together: (1) negative economic growth and (2) the duration test - \'lasting at least two quarters\' (the model answer reads \'A recession refers to a period of negative economic growth lasting at least two quarters\'). Hit both and the 7-mark element is yours; one accurate sentence is enough.',
    trap: 'The award is for the precise meaning, so a vague \'when the economy is doing badly\' or \'when unemployment rises\' scores nothing - those are symptoms, not the definition. The single most common loss is dropping the duration test: the scheme is built on \'two consecutive quarters of negative growth\' (the current-spec 2024 HL scheme words the identical concept as \'two consecutive quarters of negative growth\'). Write only \'a fall in GDP\' without the two-quarters rule and the element is incomplete.',
    source: 'LC Economics 2021 HL marking scheme, Q15(b)(i) ("Define the term recession." - "A recession refers to a period of negative economic growth lasting at least two quarters.")',
