    id: 'acc-distinguish-between-liquidity-and-solvency-hl',
    subjectId: 'accounting', subjectLabel: 'Accounting', level: 'higher',
    questionRef: '2023 HL · Q5(c)(i)',
    stem: 'Distinguish between the terms liquidity and solvency when used in ratio analysis.',
    commandWord: 'distinguish between',
    demand: 'Contrast BOTH terms on the SAME dimension — the time horizon of the debts and the test that measures it. Liquidity = ability to pay SHORT-TERM debts as they fall due (judged by the acid test ratio, which uses only liquid assets, i.e. cash and debtors, and excludes stock); solvency = ability to pay ALL debts and survive in the LONG term (a business is solvent when total assets exceed outside liabilities, judged by debt-to-equity or total-debt-to-total-assets). The distinction is short-term vs long-term, not two unrelated definitions.',
    trap: 'The scheme rewards the two terms contrasted on the shared dimension; defining only one, or describing both without flagging that liquidity is short-term and solvency is long-term, loses the comparison marks. The award answer pins liquidity to \'short-term debts as they fall due … the acid test ratio is a good indicator … includes only liquid assets i.e. cash and debtors\' and solvency to \'pay all of its debts as they fall due … total assets exceeds its outside liabilities … Debt to equity or total debt to total assets are good guides.\' Quoting a calculated ratio figure instead of contrasting the concepts scores nothing here.',
    source: 'LC Accounting 2023 HL marking scheme, Q5(c)(i) ("Liquidity measures the ability of the company to pay its short-term debts as they fall due … The acid test ratio is a good indicator of liquidity as it includes only liquid assets i.e. cash and debtors … Solvency is the ability of a company to pay all of its debts … A business is solvent if its total assets exceeds its outside liabilities")',
    marks: 5,
